1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,646
Welcome to Atomic Education.

2
00:00:02,705 --> 00:00:09,818
This lesson was written by the Atomic teaching team to help you learn Cambridge Lower Secondary Science with confidence.

3
00:00:09,818 --> 00:00:10,523
Let's begin!

4
00:00:11,582 --> 00:00:13,933
Welcome to Unit 13: Weather and Climate.

5
00:00:14,992 --> 00:00:19,048
Twenty thousand years ago, ice covered about a quarter of the Earth.

6
00:00:19,048 --> 00:00:23,986
Ice cores drilled from ancient ice show that Earth has warmed and cooled naturally.

7
00:00:23,986 --> 00:00:29,042
But today it's warming very quickly, and the evidence shows that humans are the cause.

8
00:00:30,100 --> 00:00:34,156
Weather is the temporary state of the atmosphere in a specific area.

9
00:00:34,156 --> 00:00:39,447
It includes temperature, wind, cloud cover, and precipitation: rain, snow, sleet or hail.

10
00:00:39,447 --> 00:00:41,211
It can change from day to day.

11
00:00:42,269 --> 00:00:48,265
Meteorologists forecast the weather using data from satellites, weather stations, balloons and buoys.

12
00:00:48,265 --> 00:00:52,733
Their tools include a thermometer, a rain gauge, a hygrometer for humidity,

13
00:00:52,733 --> 00:00:55,378
a wind vane and an anemometer for wind speed.

14
00:00:56,437 --> 00:00:59,435
A very hot day in the cold season is just weather.

15
00:00:59,435 --> 00:01:03,374
Climate is the usual weather pattern of a region over a long time,

16
00:01:03,374 --> 00:01:06,724
based on averages of readings taken over several decades.

17
00:01:07,783 --> 00:01:11,722
Earth is divided into climate zones, areas with a similar climate.

18
00:01:11,722 --> 00:01:17,718
They're arranged in bands depending on how far they are from the equator: equatorial, sub-equatorial,

19
00:01:17,718 --> 00:01:20,951
tropical, sub-tropical, temperate, sub-polar and polar.

20
00:01:22,010 --> 00:01:27,477
Equatorial regions are warm all year with lots of rain. Tropical regions are clear and warm.

21
00:01:27,477 --> 00:01:31,944
Temperate regions have four seasons, with rain all year and snow in winter.

22
00:01:31,944 --> 00:01:35,177
Polar regions are freezing, and their snow never melts.

23
00:01:36,236 --> 00:01:39,881
A climate graph shows the average temperature for each month,

24
00:01:39,881 --> 00:01:42,761
worked out from readings taken over thirty years.

25
00:01:43,820 --> 00:01:49,169
In this temperate city, January averages 4 degrees Celsius, and July and August average 19.

26
00:01:50,228 --> 00:01:55,636
The line rises through spring, peaks in summer and falls through autumn: four clear seasons.

27
00:01:56,695 --> 00:02:02,750
Pause and decide: if one day in January reached 15 degrees, would that change the climate of this city?

28
00:02:03,809 --> 00:02:07,865
Two identical torches shine on squared paper from the same distance.

29
00:02:07,865 --> 00:02:14,155
One points straight down; the other is tilted at an angle. Predict: which patch of light will be brighter?

30
00:02:14,155 --> 00:02:15,154
Pause and decide.

31
00:02:16,213 --> 00:02:18,329
Watch. Both torches are switched on.

32
00:02:19,388 --> 00:02:22,504
The straight-down torch makes a small, bright patch.

33
00:02:22,504 --> 00:02:26,795
The tilted torch spreads the same light over a much larger, dimmer patch.

34
00:02:27,854 --> 00:02:29,147
Explain what you saw.

35
00:02:29,147 --> 00:02:35,025
The same energy is spread over a bigger area when it arrives at an angle, so each square gets less.

36
00:02:35,025 --> 00:02:40,081
Near the equator the Sun's rays arrive almost straight down, so places there are hot.

37
00:02:40,081 --> 00:02:45,195
Near the poles they arrive at a low angle, so the energy is spread out and it is cold.

38
00:02:45,195 --> 00:02:47,488
Pause and explain it in your own words.

39
00:02:48,546 --> 00:02:54,131
Earth is a sphere, so near the equator the Sun's rays hit almost head-on and are concentrated.

40
00:02:54,131 --> 00:03:00,009
Further from the equator, the same rays are spread over a larger area, so the ground heats up less.

41
00:03:00,009 --> 00:03:03,125
The further from the equator, the cooler the climate.

42
00:03:04,184 --> 00:03:09,533
Four main factors affect climate: latitude, how far a place is from the equator; altitude,

43
00:03:09,533 --> 00:03:13,707
how high it is; relief, how flat or mountainous it is; and water mass,

44
00:03:13,707 --> 00:03:16,058
whether it's near a large body of water.

45
00:03:17,117 --> 00:03:23,348
Death Valley in the USA holds the record for the hottest temperature ever measured: 56.7 degrees Celsius.

46
00:03:23,348 --> 00:03:28,874
It lies below sea level, surrounded by mountains that trap the hot air rising from the valley.
